TIME SERIES ANALYSIS – DAILY TEMPERATURE


TASK 1

Download a public daily temperature dataset for an Indian city, load it using pandas, and plot the raw temperature data.

For this example, suppose the downloaded CSV file is named:

indian_city_temperature.csv

The CSV should contain at least these columns:

date
temperature


CODE:

import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

# Load dataset
df = pd.read_csv("indian_city_temperature.csv")

# Convert date column to datetime
df["date"] = pd.to_datetime(df["date"])

# Sort by date
df = df.sort_values("date")

# Set date as index
df.set_index("date", inplace=True)

# Display first 5 rows
print(df.head())

# Plot raw temperature data
plt.figure(figsize=(12, 5))

plt.plot(
    df.index,
    df["temperature"]
)

plt.xlabel("Date")
plt.ylabel("Temperature (°C)")
plt.title("Daily Temperature")
plt.grid()

plt.show()


EXPLANATION:

The raw time-series plot helps us visually identify long-term trends and repeating seasonal patterns. For example, Indian city temperatures generally show warmer and cooler periods during different parts of the year.


TASK 2

Decompose the temperature time series into trend, seasonal, and residual components using seasonal_decompose().

CODE:

from statsmodels.tsa.seasonal import seasonal_decompose
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

# Perform seasonal decomposition
decomposition = seasonal_decompose(
    df["temperature"],
    model="additive",
    period=365
)

# Plot all components
decomposition.plot()

plt.show()


EXPLANATION:

The decomposition separates the temperature series into:

Trend:
The long-term increase or decrease in temperature.

Seasonal:
The repeating yearly temperature pattern.

Residual:
The random variation that remains after removing trend and seasonality.

The period is set to 365 because daily temperature data usually has an annual seasonal cycle.


TASK 3

Check whether the temperature time series is stationary using the Augmented Dickey-Fuller (ADF) test.

CODE:

from statsmodels.tsa.stattools import adfuller

# Perform ADF test
result = adfuller(
    df["temperature"].dropna()
)

# Print test statistic
print("ADF Test Statistic:", result[0])

# Print p-value
print("p-value:", result[1])


# Interpret result
if result[1] < 0.05:

    print(
        "The series is likely stationary."
    )

else:

    print(
        "The series is likely non-stationary."
    )


EXPLANATION:

The ADF test checks whether the time series is stationary.

If p-value < 0.05:
The series is likely stationary.

If p-value >= 0.05:
The series is likely non-stationary.

Temperature data often contains seasonality, so the original series may be non-stationary.


TASK 4

Apply a moving average with a window size of 7 and plot the original and smoothed temperature series.

CODE:

# Calculate 7-day moving average
df["moving_average"] = (
    df["temperature"]
    .rolling(window=7)
    .mean()
)

# Plot original and smoothed data
plt.figure(figsize=(12, 5))

plt.plot(
    df.index,
    df["temperature"],
    label="Original Temperature"
)

plt.plot(
    df.index,
    df["moving_average"],
    label="7-Day Moving Average"
)

plt.xlabel("Date")
plt.ylabel("Temperature (°C)")
plt.title("Original vs 7-Day Moving Average")

plt.legend()
plt.grid()

plt.show()


EXPLANATION:

The 7-day moving average smooths short-term fluctuations and makes the underlying temperature pattern easier to observe. It reduces the effect of sudden daily changes in temperature.


TASK 5

Fit an ARIMA model and forecast the next 7 days.

Based on the ADF test and visual analysis, suppose the original series is non-stationary. We therefore use d=1.

For this simple example, we will use:

p = 1
d = 1
q = 1

Therefore:

ARIMA(1,1,1)


CODE:

from statsmodels.tsa.arima.model import ARIMA
import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

# Create ARIMA model
model = ARIMA(
    df["temperature"],
    order=(1, 1, 1)
)

# Fit model
model_fit = model.fit()

# Forecast next 7 days
forecast = model_fit.forecast(
    steps=7
)

print("Next 7 Days Temperature Forecast:")
print(forecast)


# Create future dates
future_dates = pd.date_range(
    start=df.index[-1] + pd.Timedelta(days=1),
    periods=7
)

# Plot original data
plt.figure(figsize=(12, 5))

plt.plot(
    df.index,
    df["temperature"],
    label="Original Temperature"
)

# Plot forecast
plt.plot(
    future_dates,
    forecast,
    marker="o",
    label="7-Day Forecast"
)

plt.xlabel("Date")
plt.ylabel("Temperature (°C)")
plt.title("ARIMA Temperature Forecast")

plt.legend()
plt.grid()

plt.show()


EXPLANATION:

ARIMA(1,1,1) uses one autoregressive term, one level of differencing, and one moving-average term. Differencing with d=1 helps make a non-stationary temperature series more suitable for ARIMA. The model then forecasts the temperature for the next seven days.


IMPORTANT NOTE ABOUT ARIMA:

The exact ARIMA parameters should ideally be selected after examining the data.

If the ADF test gives:

p-value < 0.05

the series is already stationary, so d can be set to 0.

If:

p-value >= 0.05

d=1 is a reasonable starting point.

The values of p and q can then be selected using ACF/PACF plots or by comparing different ARIMA models using AIC.


OPTIONAL ACF/PACF CODE:

from statsmodels.graphics.tsaplots import (
    plot_acf,
    plot_pacf
)

# Difference the series if necessary
temperature_diff = df["temperature"].diff().dropna()

# ACF
plot_acf(temperature_diff)

plt.show()

# PACF
plot_pacf(temperature_diff)

plt.show()


COMPLETE CODE

import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

from statsmodels.tsa.seasonal import seasonal_decompose
from statsmodels.tsa.stattools import adfuller
from statsmodels.tsa.arima.model import ARIMA


# ==========================================
# TASK 1: LOAD DATA
# ==========================================

df = pd.read_csv(
    "indian_city_temperature.csv"
)

df["date"] = pd.to_datetime(
    df["date"]
)

df = df.sort_values(
    "date"
)

df.set_index(
    "date",
    inplace=True
)

print(df.head())


# Plot raw temperature
plt.figure(figsize=(12, 5))

plt.plot(
    df.index,
    df["temperature"]
)

plt.xlabel("Date")
plt.ylabel("Temperature (°C)")
plt.title("Daily Temperature")

plt.grid()
plt.show()


# ==========================================
# TASK 2: DECOMPOSITION
# ==========================================

decomposition = seasonal_decompose(
    df["temperature"],
    model="additive",
    period=365
)

decomposition.plot()

plt.show()


# ==========================================
# TASK 3: ADF TEST
# ==========================================

result = adfuller(
    df["temperature"].dropna()
)

print(
    "ADF Test Statistic:",
    result[0]
)

print(
    "p-value:",
    result[1]
)

if result[1] < 0.05:

    print(
        "The series is likely stationary."
    )

else:

    print(
        "The series is likely non-stationary."
    )


# ==========================================
# TASK 4: MOVING AVERAGE
# ==========================================

df["moving_average"] = (
    df["temperature"]
    .rolling(window=7)
    .mean()
)

plt.figure(figsize=(12, 5))

plt.plot(
    df.index,
    df["temperature"],
    label="Original"
)

plt.plot(
    df.index,
    df["moving_average"],
    label="7-Day Moving Average"
)

plt.xlabel("Date")
plt.ylabel("Temperature (°C)")
plt.title("Temperature with 7-Day Moving Average")

plt.legend()
plt.grid()

plt.show()


# ==========================================
# TASK 5: ARIMA
# ==========================================

# ARIMA(1,1,1)
model = ARIMA(
    df["temperature"],
    order=(1, 1, 1)
)

model_fit = model.fit()

# Forecast next 7 days
forecast = model_fit.forecast(
    steps=7
)

print(
    "\n7-Day Temperature Forecast:"
)

print(forecast)


# Future dates
future_dates = pd.date_range(
    start=df.index[-1] +
    pd.Timedelta(days=1),
    periods=7
)


# Plot forecast
plt.figure(figsize=(12, 5))

plt.plot(
    df.index,
    df["temperature"],
    label="Original Temperature"
)

plt.plot(
    future_dates,
    forecast,
    marker="o",
    label="Forecast"
)

plt.xlabel("Date")
plt.ylabel("Temperature (°C)")
plt.title("ARIMA 7-Day Temperature Forecast")

plt.legend()
plt.grid()

plt.show()


FINAL CONCLUSION:

Time series analysis helps identify trend, seasonality, and random variation in daily temperature data. Seasonal decomposition separates these components, while the ADF test checks stationarity. A 7-day moving average smooths short-term fluctuations, and ARIMA can be used to forecast future temperatures.

For this assignment, ARIMA(1,1,1) is used as a simple starting model when the ADF test indicates that differencing is required. The exact best parameters should be selected based on the actual dataset and ADF/ACF/PACF analysis.
